paio appears headed for confirmation  this week as the country's next  Secretary of State Pompeyo will replace  Rex Tillerson who was fired by President  Trump last month so worsens  controversial tenure is part of the  story of America's shifting foreign  policy that is the subject of a new book  called war on peace the end of diplomacy  and the decline of American influence  journalist Ronan Farrow looks at the  long-term decline of the State  Department and what he calls the growing  militarization of u.s. foreign policy  Ronan Farrow spent several years working  at the State Department including the  office of the Special Representative for  Afghanistan and Pakistan last week The  New Yorker won a Pulitzer Prize for  Pharaohs coverage of Harvey Weinstein  he's a contributing writer at the  magazine Ronan Farrow joins us at the  table  Ronan good morning such a pleasure to be  here guys great to have you here have  you here you write about the decline of  the State Department that it didn't  begin in January 2017 but has been going  on for decades what did you learn  look invariably when you see the  headlines about what's happening at the  State Department now usually using words  like war on the State Department battle  against the State Department gutting of  the State Department it's usually  accompanied by unprecedented and the  fact is it's a new extreme but there are  a lot of precedents we can look at and  draw clear lessons from going back to  the Clinton administration right after  the end of the Cold War we had the  message of it's the economy stupid and a  turn inward and in fact over the course  of the 90s a 30% cut to our diplomacy  and development spending and what  happened then was our embassies around  the world became underfunded we closed a  bunch of outposts around the world and  lost a lot of influence in important  places and we actually shuttered two  government agencies related to  information and arms control clearly in  retrospect as we confront Isis and  rising nuclear powers those were goals  we didn't want to get with that American  vacuum what country is filling that  space sort of a shadowy backstory across  this whole book is that China is nipping  at our heels and filling a lot of these  spaces so don't get as important as what  is happening in today's State Department  don't get distracted from the larger  story that you spent a long time working  on help people who don't exactly know  where and why diplomacy is so important  what is so  and about diplomacy there's two ways in  which this affects our day-to-day lives  one is sort of at the top level right  there was a time in which when the  United States confronted a conflict we  had peacemakers and negotiators and  experts on these really complicated  challenges steering things you look at  us barreling into potential meetings  with North Korea leader to leader right  now at one time we had a unit of experts  that could guide that and embed it in a  long-term strategy and what the experts  were saying now is we no longer have  that we're not welcoming their views  there's a second dimension to this  though guys which is the basic kind of  unglamorous work of diplomats protects  all of us the stamping of passports the  screening of dangerous people before  they get into the country the running of  our embassies and the pulling out of  Americans who are in danger  that's all work that's being diminished  right now well you say in your book that  experience really matters that in  diplomacy persuasion is a weapon of  choice and I think you raised Ronan some  very troubling things about what's  happening in the Trump administration  you said from the very first day that it  really was the transition was a big joke  they were basically told pack your bags  and that even as we see her today there  are so many positions that have not been  filled this is very very concerning even  though this is a vast story in the sense  that it's about a fundamental shift in  how America exists in the world that we  are becoming a nation without expertise  that shoots first and asks questions  later a lot of the time it's told  through the lens of as you suggest these  characters in this ravaged profession  really bravely trying to serve our  country and getting kicked out on mass  as it turns out in the first days of the  administration which is that period  you're talking about there without being  replaced it's interesting you've talked  to all of the living Secretary of  State's on record who were very candid  with you and they all seem to be saying  the same thing which is there's a  variety of levels of candor but look  I'll give colum how was an example he  said we are ripping the guts out of  diplomacy we are mortgaging your future  so George Marshall went from World War  two general to diplomat in this what  you're writing about how much does 9/11  and the fact that the US has been  engaged in live Wars caused this shift  to a more military centric diplomacy and  what are the problems with that it's  hugely significant and I'm careful not  to oversimplify the picture here  the world landscape changed and the rise  of the national security state is partly  a consequence of these inevitable shifts  that you know we didn't necessarily have  control over but what I show is that in  conflict after conflict we actually have  gone farther than we needed to in the  direction of military dominance of the  policy process that we have actively  sabotage chances at political  settlements in places like Afghanistan  for instance does politics play a role  nobody campaigns and says I'm gonna have  more interagency groups you know they  say we're gonna defeat Isis yeah I'm  glad you raised that because partly this  is a cultural problem this is about a  group of brave men and women who served  in dangerous places for Less pay than  they could get in the private sector and  yeah there are dusty bureaucrats who you  know punch the clock and you know are  entombed in this bureaucracy and don't  get a lot done but more than that there  are wonderful men and women putting a  lot on the line and they're doing  important work that isn't celebrated  with a ticker tape parade when they come  home and I think that that's exploited  in a really vicious way for political  gain by both parties you also talked  about you had a front row seat working  with Richard Holbrooke who was not the  most popular person you could say that  on the staff but at one point he says to  you have you taken leave of your senses  never good when your boss says that to  you Ronin what had you done part of this  book with this vein popping out yeah I  forget that that's the last stretch of  his life and an incredible career on the  world stage he was a difficult  complicated person this is a really  personal book in a lot of ways it's  Parsloe partly a memoir of my time  seeing these changes firsthand as a  little guy at the bottom of the totem  pole in that administration and  Holbrooke was a great lens through which  to view this problem you know he  brokered peace in Bosnia he understood  how if military might is arrayed behind  diplomats you know no one is saying get  rid of the military downsized the  military people are saying restore the  balance in a more sensible way and he  was a great example of how that can work  if you have a correct balance of power  well tonight some time 100 list  congratulations you're on it I can't  imagine what your life has been like the  last year working on this and your New  Yorker piece my life has been very tired  very grateful for all the sources that  spoke in those stories about Weinstein  and also in this book there's a lot of  whistleblowers who put a lot on the line  to tell this story  great reporter congratulations to you  thanks guys great to be here the name of  the book is called war on peace it goes  on sale today wherever you like to buy  your books lots of places to 